<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vale of Pickering</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vale of Pickering is a low-lying plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> Moors and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds">Yorkshire Wolds</a>, drained by the River Derwent. At the end of the last Ice Age it held a glacial lake, and its former wetlands preserve major prehistoric sites, among them the Mesolithic settlement of Star Carr and the long-occupied site at West Heslerton. It is a fertile farming region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vale-of-pickering.jpg" alt="Vale of Pickering" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gordon Hatton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potato_Nab_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2991199.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
